__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"WIKI",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"WIKI_LESSON_IDS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const t={w1l1:"HTTP is stateless by design: once a response is sent, the server retains nothing about the exchange. Every subsequent request must therefore stand entirely on its own, and the server reconstructs whatever context it needs \u2014 who the user is, what they may do \u2014 from the contents of that single request.\n\nThat reconstruction is the quiet root of a large share of web vulnerabilities. The request is authored by the client, which means it is reachable by an attacker. The method, the path, the headers, the cookies, the body: each is a claim, not a fact. A server's job is to decide how much of that claim to believe, and security bugs are almost always a case of believing too much.\n\nThree concrete instincts follow. First, HTTP methods express intent, not authorization \u2014 the server, not the verb, must decide what an action is permitted to do, so treating GET as inherently safe is how state-changing endpoints get abused. Second, status codes are an information channel: a 403 where you expected a 404, or a 500 carrying a stack trace, hands an attacker a map of what exists and how the system is built. Third, identity is re-asserted on every request through forgeable carriers (cookies, tokens, headers), so the entire problem of session security is making a replayable, client-held claim trustworthy.\n\nCarry one question through the rest of the course: for any field in a request, what did the server wrongly take it to prove? Almost every chapter that follows is a specific, weaponized answer to that question.",w1l2:"Request headers feel like environment, but they are input. In a browser they are populated with sensible defaults, which lulls testers into trusting them; drive the same request from a proxy or a script and every header becomes a free-text field the attacker controls. Any decision made on a header's value is a decision made on attacker-supplied data.\n\nThe Host header is the sharpest example. Applications frequently read it to discover 'their own' domain and then build absolute URLs from it \u2014 most dangerously, password-reset links of the form https://{Host}/reset?token=\u2026 . An attacker requests a reset for the victim while sending a Host header pointing at a server they control. The application generates a genuine, valid token and emails it inside a link to the attacker's domain. When the victim clicks, the token is delivered straight to the attacker \u2014 no fake page required. The fix is structural: build absolute links from a server-configured canonical base URL, or enforce a strict allowlist of acceptable Host values, and never echo the request's Host into security-relevant output.\n\nTwo related trust mistakes round out the lesson. The Referer header causes leakage: browsers attach the full referring URL to outbound requests, so any secret carried in a query string escapes to third parties such as analytics and CDNs (and into history and logs); the defenses are a strict Referrer-Policy and keeping secrets out of URLs entirely. The User-Agent \u2014 and any similar self-description \u2014 is freely spoofable, so gating access, features, or security checks on it is bypassed the instant the attacker edits the string.\n\nThe through-line is one rule: a header is input, so derive security-critical values from server state, not from the request's description of itself.",w1l3:"HTTP/1.1 announces how long a message is using text fields \u2014 Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding \u2014 and when those signals disagree, each server in the path must decide how to interpret the bytes. Those judgment calls are the soil that HTTP request smuggling grows in. HTTP/2 was, in part, a fix: it frames messages in binary with explicit, unambiguous lengths, removing the guesswork.\n\nThe trouble is that a real request traverses a chain. An HTTP/2 edge server typically downgrades the request to HTTP/1.1 in order to talk to a back-end origin, and that translation drags the old ambiguity back in. The vulnerable assumption is that the front-end and back-end agree on exactly where one request ends and the next begins. When they don't, bytes the front-end attributes to one request are parsed by the back-end as the beginning of the next \u2014 and the attacker's leftover prefix is prepended to whichever user's request comes through next.\n\nThat prefix is the payload: depending on the target it can capture another user's request, poison a shared cache so other users are served attacker content, or bypass front-end access controls because the back-end now reads traffic through the attacker's framing. The mechanics of building these requests are a later chapter \u2014 Semester 4 covers desync in depth \u2014 but the goal here is to recognize the seam.\n\nThe durable lesson is about boundaries. A protocol upgrade rarely retires the old parser; it layers a new one on top, and vulnerabilities cluster wherever two parsers with different rules meet. Defenders prefer HTTP/2 end to end, normalize or reject ambiguous framing at the edge, and avoid reusing a single back-end connection across different users, which is what lets one user's smuggled prefix reach another.",w2l1:"Encoding is the act of re-representing bytes so they survive a particular channel \u2014 percent-encoding for URLs, entities for HTML, Base64 for binary-in-text, escapes for Unicode. None of it changes what a value means; it changes how the value is written. Security trouble starts when the written form and the meaning drift apart as the value travels through a request, because the code that inspects the value and the code that acts on it can end up looking at different forms.\n\nThe defining mistake is validate-then-decode. A filter runs at one point and judges the bytes it can see there; if any later stage decodes those bytes, the filter evaluated a disguise rather than the payload that reaches the sink. The attacker's job is simply to encode a blocked payload just enough that the filter's pattern no longer matches, then rely on a downstream decode \u2014 in the framework, the database driver, the template engine, the file API \u2014 to restore it before it is used.\n\nThe fix is to invert the order: canonicalize first. Fully decode the input to a single canonical form for the context it is headed to, validate that canonical form, and only then use it. Equally important, refuse to act on input that is still encoded after canonicalization, which is a strong signal that someone is trying to smuggle a form past your checks.\n\nHold this as the lens for the whole week: the bug is never the encoding itself, it is the gap between the filter's view of the data and the sink's view. Close that gap and the rest of the week's tricks stop working.",w2l2:"Percent-encoding writes a byte as %XX, and every component that handles a URL \u2014 the browser, a proxy, a load balancer, the application framework \u2014 may decode it. The decisive detail is how many times a given value is decoded along the path, and whether the component that validates it decodes the same number of times as the component that ultimately consumes it. When those counts differ, a filter can be made to inspect a value at one decode depth while the sink reads it at another.\n\nDouble encoding weaponizes that difference. Encode the payload once, then encode the result again: a dot becomes %2e, and %2e becomes %252e. A filter that decodes a single time and validates sees %2e%2e%2f, which is not the string '../' it was told to block, so it passes the request. A downstream component then decodes a second time, turning %2e back into a dot, and the path traversal it was supposed to stop is reconstructed after the check has already succeeded.\n\nThe same shape appears beyond path traversal \u2014 blocked keywords, protocol identifiers, and routing tokens can all be hidden one extra layer deep when a control assumes a fixed decode depth. The attacker simply adds one more layer than the control removes.\n\nThe defense is to decode to a fixed point: keep decoding until the value stops changing, and validate only that fully-resolved form. Alternatively, reject any input that is still encoded after a single canonical decode. Either way, the rule is to validate at the same depth the sink consumes, so the filter and the sink can never disagree about what the value is.",w2l3:"The previous lessons established one gap \u2014 validate in one form, consume in another. This lesson widens it: a single logical character has many legal spellings, and each consuming context decodes its own. A less-than sign can be written as &lt;, &#60;, &#x3c;, %3C, or a Unicode escape, and a blocklist that recognizes one of those is blind to the others.\n\nHTML entity decoding is the canonical example. A server that scans response or input text for the literal string <script> will not find it in &#x3c;script&#x3e;, but the browser's HTML parser decodes the entity form back into a real tag at parse time \u2014 precisely after the server's check has passed. Base64 generalizes the idea to whole payloads: a blocklist staring at a Base64 string sees only opaque text, so an application that decodes the value and forwards the bytes to a sink without re-validating them has inspected ciphertext rather than the real input. Unicode is the most subtle case, because normalization forms such as NFKC and case-folding map distinct code points onto the same character after validation has run; a string that passed a check can normalize into a dangerous one downstream, and two strings that look different can be treated as equal.\n\nAcross HTML, Base64, and Unicode the defense does not change. Decode or normalize the value to a single canonical form for the context it is bound for, validate that form, and re-validate anything you decode before it reaches a sink. The encodings are just different alphabets for the same underlying mistake, and canonicalization-before-use is the single rule that closes all of them.",w3l1:"A browser is best understood as a runtime that executes untrusted code from many mutually distrusting parties simultaneously, all while holding the user's cookies, sessions, and saved credentials. Its central security responsibility is therefore isolation: keeping one party's code from observing or acting on another's data.\n\nThe principal that isolation is built around is the origin \u2014 the triple of scheme, host, and port. https://app.com, http://app.com, and https://app.com:8443 are three distinct origins, each with its own walled-off storage and DOM access. Neither the registrable domain nor the individual page is the boundary; the origin is. Modern browsers reinforce this with a multi-process model, placing different sites in different operating-system processes so that a memory-safety bug or a speculative-execution side channel in one cannot trivially reach another.\n\nTwo engines do the dangerous work. The rendering engine parses HTML and CSS, and the JavaScript engine executes script \u2014 both operating on bytes delivered straight from the network and running inside the user's authenticated session. This is why parser ambiguities and script sinks are treated as security defects rather than cosmetic issues: they are the points where attacker-controlled input becomes execution.\n\nEvery control in the rest of this week answers one question: may code from origin A observe or act on origin B's data? Same-origin enforcement, content policies, and cookie attributes are all rules layered on that question, and the bugs are the exceptions to those rules.",w3l2:"The Same-Origin Policy (SOP) is the browser's default-deny rule for cross-origin reads: script running in one origin may not read the responses of another. It is the floor on which every other browser control is built, raising or lowering what is permitted.\n\nThe crucial property is that SOP is asymmetric. It restricts reading far more than sending. The browser will happily issue many cross-origin requests \u2014 form submissions, image and script loads \u2014 and it will attach the user's cookies to them; what it withholds is the ability for script to read the response. 'Can send, cannot read' is not a quirk but the defining shape of the policy, and it directly explains two later subjects: cross-site request forgery exploits the ability to send a state-changing request without needing to read anything, and Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) exists precisely to grant a controlled, server-opted-in exception to the read restriction.\n\nThe assumption worth probing as an attacker is that SOP protects a given piece of data at all. It protects script-readable responses, not every interaction. Cross-origin embeds run, cross-origin posts land, and side channels \u2014 request timing, response size, the firing of load or error events \u2014 can leak the very bits SOP was meant to conceal.\n\nFor a defender, the discipline is to treat SOP as a floor rather than a wall: enumerate what it does not block, and configure CORS as a strict allowlist of known origins rather than reflecting whatever Origin header arrives (especially when credentials are allowed).",w3l3:"Content Security Policy (CSP) is a response header that constrains which sources of script, style, and other resources the browser will load and execute for a page. It is essential to frame it correctly: CSP does not fix an injection vulnerability, it limits what an injected payload can accomplish once present. It is defense-in-depth layered on top of the real fix, which is correct output encoding.\n\nThe value is in shrinking the blast radius. With a strict policy, an attacker who manages to inject markup may still find their inline script refused execution and their exfiltration endpoint refused loading. The two failure modes that hollow out real-world policies are predictable: 'unsafe-inline', which re-permits exactly the inline script the policy was meant to stop, and over-broad source allowlists, where a single allowlisted CDN that happens to host a JSONP endpoint or an outdated library becomes the attacker's route back to script execution.\n\nA sound policy avoids unsafe-inline and unsafe-eval and instead authorizes inline script by nonce or hash: each legitimate script carries a per-response random nonce (or matches a known hash), and injected script, unable to supply it, does not run. The techniques for bypassing weak policies are developed in Semester 2; the goal here is to recognize what a strong policy looks like.\n\nThe defender's takeaway: pair CSP with output encoding, prefer nonces or hashes over unsafe-inline, and remember that every allowlisted origin is code you have chosen to trust.",w3l4:"After the broad policies come the per-feature controls, and for sessions the most important live on the cookie itself. A cookie is attached automatically to matching requests, so its security is largely a function of its attributes. Secure restricts it to HTTPS, preventing plaintext leakage. HttpOnly removes it from the JavaScript-readable surface, so an injected script cannot read the session out of document.cookie. SameSite governs whether the cookie accompanies cross-site requests, which is the primary lever against cross-site request forgery. A session cookie missing HttpOnly or Secure is a finding on sight.\n\nThree more controls tighten the surrounding walls. The iframe sandbox attribute strips an embedded frame to zero capability by default \u2014 no script, no forms, no top-level navigation \u2014 and re-grants individual capabilities only as explicit tokens, which is least privilege for untrusted embeds. Subresource Integrity (SRI) pins a cryptographic hash to a script or stylesheet loaded from a third party, so a compromised CDN serving altered bytes is rejected by the browser. HSTS instructs the browser to use HTTPS for the domain for a set duration, closing the brief downgrade-to-http window an attacker would otherwise use to strip TLS.\n\nNone of these are exotic, and that is the point: they are the knobs that decide whether the isolation described all week is actually enforced. Set cookie flags correctly, sandbox untrusted iframes, pin third-party code with SRI, and enable HSTS \u2014 each closes a specific, well-documented attack path, and their absence is what turns a theoretical boundary into a real one for the attacker.",w4l1:"The Same-Origin Policy is better pictured as a wall with sanctioned doors than as a solid barrier. CORS, postMessage, document.domain, and legacy mechanisms such as JSONP all exist to let origins interact deliberately. Understanding those doors is the prerequisite for finding the ones left unlatched, because real-world bypasses almost never come from the policy being absent.\n\nThe vocabulary matters. An exception is a deliberate, opted-in relaxation; a bypass is an unintended cross-origin read or action the model never meant to permit. Both produce the same observable effect \u2014 code in origin A touches origin B \u2014 but one is configured and the other is a defect, and only the latter is a finding.\n\nMost bypasses cluster into a handful of classes: a misconfigured exception such as a CORS policy that reflects any Origin; a confused deputy where the browser acts on the wrong origin's behalf; a parser or normalization mismatch where two components disagree about what the origin is; and a UI or context confusion where the human is misled about which origin they are on. The attacker's opening move on any target is therefore an inventory of the doors \u2014 which exceptions are enabled, how loosely, and whether any layer derives the origin differently.\n\nFor a defender, the discipline is to treat every enabled relaxation as owned attack surface and to audit each with a single question: could this be driven by an origin I did not intend? Answering it honestly turns a stretched exception back into a wall.",w4l2:"Old vulnerabilities are worth studying even when the specific bug is long patched, because the detail changes while the shape recurs. Two classic Same-Origin Policy bypasses illustrate the shape rather than offer anything to reuse. In CVE-2007-0981, a Firefox-era flaw, a null byte truncating location.hostname confused the browser about a URI's host, so content was treated as belonging to a different origin than it actually did \u2014 a host-parsing disagreement that promoted attacker-controlled content into a trusted origin's context. In CVE-2011-3246, a flaw in Apple's CFNetwork, a crafted URL was parsed improperly, so the browser navigated to \u2014 and transmitted its cookies to \u2014 an unintended site: the networking layer resolved the URL's origin one way while its true origin was another.\n\nThe common root is the important part: in neither case did SOP 'turn off.' The policy was enforced the entire time. The bug was that one layer computed the origin one way and another layer computed it differently, and the gap between those two answers became a cross-origin compromise. This is precisely the parser-disagreement instinct developed earlier in the course \u2014 front-end versus back-end in request smuggling, filter depth versus sink depth in double encoding \u2014 now pointed at origins.\n\nThe research takeaway follows directly: when hunting SOP issues, do not look for a missing check, look for two components that derive the origin independently. Any point where the origin is computed or associated more than once is a point where the two computations can disagree, and that disagreement is the vulnerability.",w4l3:"Site isolation exists to answer a worst-case threat model. If the renderer process running untrusted web code is fully compromised, or if a hardware side channel such as Spectre lets that process speculatively read its own memory, then any other site's data sitting in the same process is exposed. The defense is structural rather than clever: give each site its own operating-system process, so that another site's data was never co-resident to be read in the first place.\n\nTwo qualifications define its scope. First, the unit of isolation is the site \u2014 the registrable domain plus scheme \u2014 which is broader than the origin. sub.a.com and a.com are different origins but the same site, so cross-origin-same-site data may still share a process; site isolation hardens the enforcement of SOP without replacing SOP's finer granularity. Second, it is specifically a defense against memory disclosure. It stops a compromised renderer or a speculative read from reaching another site's HTML, cookies, or storage, because that data is elsewhere.\n\nWhat it does not address is equally important: SOP-level logic bugs, CORS misconfiguration, phishing, and network-position attacks all deliver data through legitimate channels, and no process boundary helps with them. The practical consequence for a tester is to treat site isolation as a strong mitigation for one frightening class of bug while continuing to reason about a target's access control at the origin and policy layer, where the everyday vulnerabilities actually live. 'It's isolated' is not a substitute for 'the policy is correct.'",w4l4:"The fitting close to a chapter on the browser's security model is to attack the assumption the entire model rests on: that the user knows which origin they are interacting with, and learns it by reading the address bar. The address bar is the human trust anchor, and address-bar spoofing attacks it directly while every policy control continues to function perfectly.\n\nThe primitives vary. A navigation or rendering race can display the target's URL while attacker content remains on screen. A slow-load or interstitial trick can leave a trusted URL showing over the wrong page. A long, right-to-left, or Unicode-laden URL can push the real host out of the visible field. The most durable variant does not even lie: an internationalized-domain (IDN) homograph registers a domain whose characters are visually identical to a trusted brand \u2014 a Cyrillic letter standing in for a Latin one \u2014 so the bar is perfectly honest and the human's eyes are deceived.\n\nThe browser's anti-phishing layer is part of the same attack surface. Reputation and blocklist checks can be evaded with a brand-new domain, with cloaking that serves a benign page to the safety crawler while showing the attack to victims, or with a redirect laundered through a trusted host; defeat that layer and the last warning never fires. A convincing spoof plus a clean reputation check is a complete phish.\n\nThe lesson for defenders and users alike is that the address bar and the anti-phishing layer are security UI with their own bypass surface. 'It shows the right URL' is necessary but not sufficient, and security decisions should never be rendered somewhere a spoof can reach them.",w5l1:"Reconnaissance rests on a single premise: a target's real attack surface is everything it exposes, not the handful of hosts its own team keeps in mind. Acquisitions, abandoned projects, and shadow IT leave behind machines nobody decommissions, and the asset that ends up compromised is usually the one missing from the official inventory. Recon, in effect, builds the inventory the defender never finished.\n\nThat is why the work starts from ownership rather than from known hostnames. An organization controls one or more Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs) and the IP prefixes announced under them, and that public routing and registration data draws the outer boundary of what the organization is responsible for. From a single known domain you can pivot to its IP, to the netblock that IP sits in, and to the ASN that announces the block; from the ASN you expand back out to every prefix it covers, then sweep those prefixes for hosts that answer.\n\nThe defender's response is to run the identical exercise inwardly. Continuous asset discovery seeded from your own ASNs and blocks finds the forgotten host before an outsider does, which is the only way to remove the free entry point. You cannot protect what you have not enumerated, and the attacker's first advantage is simply having enumerated more thoroughly than the owner.",w5l2:"A single IP address is rarely a single website. Shared hosting and name-based virtual hosting routinely place many domains on one address, so an IP should be read as a neighborhood rather than a house. Reverse IP lookup converts an address into the list of domains co-located on it, and that list expands the surface: from one in-scope host you surface its infrastructure neighbors, any of which \u2014 if weak \u2014 can become the route into the shared environment.\n\nRecon at this stage is fundamentally a scale problem. A real estate involves thousands of addresses and millions of candidate names, which is why lookups and probes are threaded and run concurrently; a serial sweep simply never finishes inside a useful window. Concurrency is a means, not a flourish.\n\nTwo judgments keep the breadth honest. First, scope: co-located domains may belong to unrelated tenants and fall outside the engagement, so discovery is not authorization. Second, noise: aggressive concurrency trips rate limits and intrusion detection and can return inconsistent data, so the rate is tuned rather than maximized. For the defender, the lesson is to assume IPs are shared and enumerable and to treat 'unlisted' as meaningless \u2014 anything that answers on an in-range address is discoverable and must be inventoried and hardened accordingly.",w5l3:"Once a target's addresses and names are mapped, the next question is the literal listening surface: which ports respond and what runs behind them. An open port is a service, and a service is code that will hold a conversation with anyone who connects. Conceptually this proceeds in two stages: a mass port scanner sweeps large IP ranges quickly to find what is listening, and an HTTP prober then determines which of those listeners are actually web applications worth deeper attention.\n\nThe decisive skill is not operating the tools but interpreting their output. A 200 versus a 401 versus a connection reset, a server banner, a redirect to a login page \u2014 each signal narrows thousands of open ports to the few that are genuinely interesting. The tooling produces data; the analyst produces the shortlist, and the quality of the engagement is mostly the quality of that reduction.\n\nScanning is also a continuous tradeoff between rate and breadth on one side and stealth and accuracy on the other. Push too hard and you trip rate limits and detection or simply collect inconsistent results; move too cautiously and the sweep never completes. The scan is tuned to the estate. For defenders the implication is blunt: everything that listens is enumerable from outside, so the real defense is to listen less \u2014 close unneeded ports, gate services behind authentication and allowlists, and never treat an obscure port as protection.",w5l4:"There is a clean line in recon between knowing that a service is present and knowing exactly what it is. Crossing it is the pivot of the whole discipline. An open service is only potential, but a precisely versioned service \u2014 a specific web server release, a specific CMS version \u2014 maps directly to that release's known vulnerabilities, letting an attacker skip straight to what is already broken.\n\nVersions leak through many channels: Server and X-Powered-By headers, default error pages, characteristic file paths, JavaScript bundle hashes, and subtle behavioral differences between releases. Forcing a service into an error frequently discloses more than its normal responses do. The pivot itself is mechanical: fingerprint the product and pin the exact release, then map that release to public vulnerability data such as CVEs and vendor advisories. Recon ends precisely where 'which known issues apply here' begins.\n\nPrecision cuts both ways. A wrong version wastes effort on exploits that cannot apply, and defenders sometimes strip or falsify version strings, so a banner should be corroborated with behavior before it is trusted. The defensive takeaway is the mirror image of the technique: do not advertise versions. Strip identifying headers, normalize error pages, and patch, because the entire method lives in the gap between the moment a version is disclosed and the moment a patch is applied.",w6l1:"Subdomains multiply the attack surface because each one is typically a distinct host running a distinct application \u2014 staging, admin, legacy, vendor integrations \u2014 and organizations create them far more readily than they retire them. The subdomain tree is therefore where forgotten and weaker applications accumulate, which is exactly where an attacker wants to look.\n\nDiscovery divides into two modes. Passive enumeration mines records that already exist elsewhere \u2014 Certificate Transparency logs, search engines, public DNS datasets \u2014 without ever sending a packet to the target. Active enumeration queries DNS directly, brute-forcing candidate names to learn which resolve. Certificate Transparency deserves special mention: because every issued TLS certificate is written to a public log, subdomain names (including internal-sounding ones) become public the instant a certificate is issued, with no scanning required.\n\nThe two modes trade off against each other. Passive is stealthy, fast, and broad, but it can only surface what has been recorded somewhere; active finds names that were never published anywhere but is noisier and touches the target's resolvers. Serious recon runs both and merges the results. For defenders the consequence is stark: assume every subdomain you have ever certified is public knowledge, inventory the whole tree, and retire dead entries \u2014 a forgotten dev subdomain is a production-grade entry point.",w6l2:"Active subdomain enumeration is brute force against DNS: a wordlist of candidate names is resolved to see which exist. The available tooling is fast, but speed is not the difficult part \u2014 trusting the answers is, because DNS will readily return results that are not real.\n\nThe canonical trap is wildcard DNS. A domain configured with a wildcard record resolves anything.target.com, so a naive brute force reports millions of subdomains that do not exist. Sound enumeration detects the wildcard and discards names that resolve only because of it. Resolvers introduce a second class of error: public resolvers can be poisoned, rate-limited, or simply return junk, so results are validated against a vetted resolver list (the role of a tool such as DNSValidator) and confirmed hits are re-checked. A name counts as real only when trusted resolvers agree it is.\n\nThe deliverable of this stage is therefore not 'names that returned an answer' but 'names confirmed to exist after wildcard and resolver validation.' Skipping that step floods the engagement with false positives that waste every subsequent stage. For defenders, the takeaway is that brute force reliably finds predictable names like dev, vpn, and jira, so obscure naming provides no protection; the only real control is ensuring that every name which resolves points to something inventoried and hardened.",w6l3:"The most valuable subdomains are often absent from any wordlist because they leak directly from an application's own configuration. A Content Security Policy header, in order to function, enumerates the domains a site trusts for scripts, frames, and connections \u2014 effectively a curated list of related hosts published in plain sight. Favicon hashing approaches the problem from the opposite direction: hashing a site's favicon and querying internet-wide scan datasets for every host serving the same icon ties together an organization's scattered or deliberately differently-named assets.\n\nThese techniques, combined with passive sources and active brute force, produce overlapping and inconsistent lists, which is why aggregation tools such as Amass exist \u2014 they fuse many sources into a single normalized, deduplicated set of real hosts. Consolidation is genuine analytical work rather than a formatting step: the same host appears under multiple names, with and without ports, behind CDNs and redirect chains, and reducing all of that to distinct, live assets is what converts raw output into a workable target list.\n\nFor defenders, the uncomfortable point is that CSP headers and favicons are recon signals that cannot really be hidden, so the defense is upstream: keep the asset inventory tight, because anything that points back to the organization \u2014 a trusted domain in a policy, a shared icon \u2014 is a thread an attacker will pull.",w6l4:"Subdomain takeover is frequently the payoff of thorough enumeration. It arises when a DNS record \u2014 most often a CNAME \u2014 still points to a third-party service such as a cloud storage bucket, a platform-as-a-service application, or a SaaS page that has been deleted or was never claimed. The dangling record is the vulnerability: DNS asserts that blog.target.com is a particular hosted app, but the app no longer exists and the underlying name is free to register.\n\nClaiming that resource gives the attacker control of what loads at the subdomain, and because the subdomain is genuinely part of the victim's domain, the impact is same-site by definition. That enables phishing on a trusted name, capture of cookies scoped to the parent domain, satisfaction of OAuth and redirect allowlists, and bypass of any control that trusts '*.target.com'. It is not a lookalike domain; it is the real subdomain.\n\nConfirmation is careful, non-destructive work. A CNAME pointing at an unclaimed service usually exhibits a provider-specific tell \u2014 a particular 404 or a 'no such app' fingerprint \u2014 and the tester verifies that the record is genuinely dangling and the resource is claimable rather than defacing a live service. For defenders, takeover is fundamentally a lifecycle bug: when a cloud resource is torn down, its DNS record must be removed first, and standing CNAMEs pointing at services the organization no longer owns should be audited, because each dangling record is a trusted-origin host waiting to be claimed.",w7l1:"The pages a site links to are only the portion it intends to present. The real attack surface includes everything that responds to a request but is not linked anywhere: administrative panels, superseded API versions, backup archives, debug and health endpoints, and leftover test pages. Directory and content fuzzing surfaces these by brute force \u2014 requesting a wordlist of likely paths such as /admin, /api/v1, /.git, or /backup.zip and observing which return something other than 'not found.'\n\nThe technique is productive precisely because unlinked endpoints are so often the unprotected ones. A developer who 'hides' an administrative route by not linking it frequently never applies authentication to it, treating obscurity as a control; fuzzing dismantles that assumption in seconds. As with scanning, interpreting the output is the real skill \u2014 distinguishing a genuine 200 from a soft-404 that returns 200 with an error body, recognizing a 403 as proof that a path exists but is gated, and following redirects. Status and body together establish whether a result is a real discovery.\n\nThe defensive conclusion is unambiguous: assume every path is reachable and enumerable. Declining to link a route protects nothing, because the route still answers; the only effective protection is authentication and authorization enforced on the route itself, applied uniformly across the application.",w7l2:"If endpoints are doors, parameters are the questions asked through them, and each parameter \u2014 whether in the query string, request body, a header, a cookie, or a path segment \u2014 is a distinct point at which attacker input meets application logic, and therefore a distinct place a vulnerability can live. Applications routinely honor parameters that appear in no documentation: debug flags, feature toggles, legacy fields the code still reads, and alternate names a framework happens to accept. Parameter discovery brute-forces and infers these so that the tester exercises inputs the developers have forgotten remain live.\n\nCrawling and spidering tools such as Gospider construct the link graph by following hyperlinks, forms, and URLs referenced from JavaScript, converting an opaque site into an indexed set of endpoints and the parameters they carry. Because applications change with every deploy \u2014 a new endpoint here, a new API referenced by an updated bundle there \u2014 surface mapping is run continuously rather than once, so that surface appearing after the initial pass is still caught.\n\nThe defensive point follows directly: every parameter the code reads is attack surface regardless of whether it is documented, and dead or legacy parameters the code still honors are pure liability. If it is parsed, it will be tested, so unused inputs should be removed rather than left quietly accepted.",w7l3:"Effective testing begins by identifying what an application is built from \u2014 the web server, the application framework, the CMS, the front-end libraries, and any web application firewall in front of it \u2014 because the stack dictates which classes of bug are plausible and which known issues are worth checking first. Technology fingerprinting reads the tells a stack cannot help emitting: response headers, cookie names (a framework's default session cookie is a giveaway), default error pages, characteristic file paths, and front-end asset hashes, with tools such as WhatWeb and Wappalyzer automating the matching.\n\nForcing errors is the highest-yield technique, because a malformed request or a request for a non-existent resource frequently returns a stack trace, a version string, or a framework's distinctive error page that the normal happy path conceals. Fingerprinting is a focusing step rather than an end in itself: establishing that a target is a specific CMS at a specific version behind a specific WAF tells the tester which payloads to attempt, which known issues apply, and which evasions will be required, turning a broad target into a concrete plan.\n\nDefensively, the goal is to minimize what the stack announces \u2014 stripping framework headers and telltale cookies, replacing default error pages, and avoiding version-specific paths \u2014 because every tell removed forces an attacker to guess where they could otherwise simply know.",w7l4:"The final stage of reconnaissance before bespoke testing is to check everything discovered against everything already known to be broken. Templated vulnerability scanners, of which Nuclei is the archetype, run a large library of signatures \u2014 one per known issue \u2014 across the entire list of discovered hosts. Each template encodes a known issue as a request-and-match: send a particular request, look for a particular response, and thereby flag an exposed administrative panel, a default credential, a specific CVE, an information leak, or a misconfiguration. This is the version-to-CVE pivot from Week 5, automated across the whole estate.\n\nThe value lies in scale and freshness: thousands of checks against thousands of hosts, with the template set updated as new issues are published, so that what was once a manual 'is this the vulnerable version?' becomes an immediate sweep for the low-hanging fruit. The unavoidable caveat is false positives. A signature can match coincidentally, and a flagged issue may be patched while still fingerprinting as vulnerable, or simply be wrong. Reconnaissance flags candidates; the tester confirms before claiming, treating a finding as a lead rather than a conclusion.\n\nThe defensive mirror is sobering: attackers run the same public templates against an organization the day a CVE is disclosed, so the exposure window is the gap between disclosure and patching. Running the same scanners inwardly and treating any 'exposed and known-vulnerable' result as already under attack is the only way to close that window first.",w8l1:"Cloud object storage \u2014 Amazon S3 buckets and their equivalents on other platforms \u2014 occupies a single global namespace populated with human-predictable names such as companyname-backups, companyname-assets, and companyname-dev. Predictability makes the namespace enumerable, and enumeration proceeds two ways: guessing from wordlists seeded with the organization's name and common suffixes, and harvesting bucket references the application leaks through asset URLs in HTML and JavaScript, redirects, error messages, and mobile app bundles.\n\nA bucket's existence can be confirmed even without read access, because the provider returns distinct responses for a name that does not exist versus one that exists but denies access. Enumeration therefore establishes which candidate names are real before any attempt to read their contents. This matters because buckets routinely store backups, credentials, customer data, and build artifacts; a single over-permissive bucket can disclose far more than any vulnerability in the application itself, and it frequently sits entirely outside the app's own security boundary.\n\nThe defensive posture follows directly: treat bucket names as discoverable rather than secret, derive all security from the access policy rather than from name obscurity, and avoid leaking bucket references in client-side code where harvesting them is trivial.",w8l2:"A bucket is exactly as safe as the access configuration attached to it \u2014 the ACL and the bucket policy that define who may read, write, and list its contents. Misconfiguration here is the entire vulnerability, because the data is only ever one permission away from public. Three holes recur: public read, which lets anyone download the contents; public list, which lets anyone enumerate every object key and so converts 'I need the filename' into a complete index; and public write, which lets anyone replace objects.\n\nPublic write is the case practitioners most often underrate. If a site serves its JavaScript or other assets from a bucket and that bucket is world-writable, an attacker who overwrites those assets obtains stored cross-site scripting on every page that loads them \u2014 a storage misconfiguration escalating directly into a supply-chain compromise. Identifying these holes is a matter of reasoning rather than tooling: a policy can permit an action through an overly broad principal, a wildcard resource, or a missing condition, and the policy is read with the same question used for every access-control bug \u2014 what does this permit that it should not?\n\nDefensively the rules are familiar: default-deny, least privilege, block public access at the account level, and rigorously separate read from write. Above all, never serve executable assets from a bucket that anyone can write to.",w8l3:"One cloud misconfiguration is subtle enough to look careful while being catastrophic: granting a bucket, or any resource, to the provider's 'Authenticated Users' group. The label suggests restriction \u2014 access requires being logged in \u2014 but on the major platforms it means any account holder on the entire platform, not anyone within the organization. Because anyone can create a free account in minutes, 'Authenticated Users' is effectively public with one trivial extra step.\n\nThe underlying error is the conflation of authentication with authorization. Authentication establishes identity; authorization decides permission; granting to 'Authenticated Users' answers the first question when it should answer the second. This is not a niche cloud quirk but a specific instance of a general root cause that recurs throughout the course \u2014 scoping access to 'is logged in' rather than 'is allowed to do this.' Exploitation is mundane by design: register for the platform, present valid credentials, and the resource treats the attacker as trusted, with no cryptographic flaw or crafted payload involved.\n\nThe defensive rule is correspondingly simple and absolute: never grant to broad, platform-wide groups. Scope grants to specific principals \u2014 named roles and accounts \u2014 and specific actions, and read every grant as authorization to perform exactly one thing, never as a welcome to any party that happens to be authenticated.",w8l4:"The recon half of Semester 1 is best understood not as a collection of tools but as a single compounding pipeline. Ownership data \u2014 ASNs and announced IP blocks \u2014 establishes the scope. Names (subdomains) and hosts (open ports and services) populate that scope. Surface mapping (endpoints and parameters) and fingerprinting describe each application in turn. Templated scanning flags whatever is already known to be broken, and cloud enumeration captures the assets that live outside the application entirely. Each stage produces the input to the next.\n\nThe compounding is the point. A subdomain discovered in Week 6 becomes a host to scan; its service version pivots to a known issue; its unlinked admin panel was surfaced by fuzzing; its referenced bucket was enumerated and its policy read. The value of reconnaissance lies in these connections rather than in any single finding. Three disciplines keep the pipeline honest across all of it: breadth, because the forgotten asset is so often the way in; validation, because wildcards, false positives, and dangling records all lie; and prioritization, because the highest-impact, most-exposed surface should be worked first.\n\nReconnaissance ends where exploitation begins, with a validated, prioritized map of the target's real attack surface \u2014 annotated with stack, versions, exposed endpoints, and known issues. That map is precisely the input to Semester 2, where the work shifts from describing the surface to turning it into access. The strongest defense is to run this entire pipeline inwardly and continuously, because whoever maps the surface first sets the terms."},s=new Set(["w10l1","w10l2","w10l3","w10l4","w11l1","w11l2","w11l3","w11l4","w12l1","w12l2","w12l3","w12l4","w13l1","w13l2","w13l3","w13l4","w14l1","w14l2","w14l3","w14l4","w15l1","w15l2","w15l3","w15l4","w16l1","w16l2","w16l3","w16l4","w17l1","w17l2","w17l3","w17l4","w18l1","w18l2","w18l3","w18l4","w19l1","w19l2","w19l3","w19l4","w1l1","w1l2","w1l3","w20l1","w20l2","w20l3","w20l4","w21l1","w21l2","w21l3","w21l4","w22l1","w22l2","w22l3","w22l4","w23l1","w23l2","w23l3","w23l4","w24l1","w24l2","w24l3","w24l4","w25l1","w25l2","w25l3","w25l4","w26l1","w26l2","w26l3","w26l4","w27l1","w27l2","w27l3","w27l4","w28l1","w28l2","w28l3","w28l4","w29l1","w29l2","w29l3","w29l4","w2l1","w2l2","w2l3","w30l1","w30l2","w30l3","w30l4","w31l1","w31l2","w31l3","w31l4","w32l1","w32l2","w32l3","w32l4","w33l1","w33l2","w33l3","w33l4","w34l1","w34l2","w34l3","w34l4","w35l1","w35l2","w35l3","w35l4","w36l1","w36l2","w36l3","w36l4","w37l1","w37l2","w37l3","w37l4","w38l1","w38l2","w38l3","w38l4","w39l1","w39l2","w39l3","w39l4","w39l5","w3l1","w3l2","w3l3","w3l4","w40l1","w40l2","w40l3","w40l4","w4l1","w4l2","w4l3","w4l4","w5l1","w5l2","w5l3","w5l4","w6l1","w6l2","w6l3","w6l4","w7l1","w7l2","w7l3","w7l4","w8l1","w8l2","w8l3","w8l4","w9l1","w9l2","w9l3","w9l4"])},505,[]);
